Assignment 1.05 - ncurses

Hello TA,

This version of the pokegame implements ncurses! I reserve the 1st line on the screen for messages, and the last 2 for status effects (to be used later). The rest of the screen remains as the "actual map". The player is a teal color, while trainers are red. Everything else is pretty self explanatory. I also removed the old sleep() method to run continuously from 1.04 and shifted to ncurses getch() to await user input to "proceed" the game? Do a turn? You know what Im trying to say. The player can be controlled using vi cursor movement, use '>' to enter any poke-store, use 'q' or 'Q' to quit, use 't' to pull up a menu of all trainers on screen, and ' ' to wait (thats a space).

The player can engage in WIP battles with trainers, simply defeat them with the all powerful 'esc' key. Trainers will no longer pursue the player or be interacted with when defeated, and the player may move over them. I made this decision to prevent the player from softlocking themselves, i.e. imagine the scenario of a path over water or through a mountain. If a trainer dies on that tile, they could block the player from passing them. Therefore I allow the player to move on and over the defeated trainer. I think this is what the assignment description is referring to when it says "nothing happens", but figured I should explain myself here too. The player will also not be able to move into any illegal cells, (that is borders, boulders, trees, etc.) or gates to transition maps (as stated in the assinment desc.).

In addition, all key presses require NO HITTING of the enter key, completely unbuffered. :)

There is a bug (I think) surrounding the esc key. It takes 1 second longer to process compared to other inputs. I am not sure of the source.

NEVERMIND! ncurses looks to see if you are going to press another key after esc, and it has a timeout of 1 sec to confirm that. Might be good to pass on to other students. I fixed it by using set_escdelay(25);

Anyways, thanks!

If there are any questions feel free to reach me at griffinu@iastate.edu


